RS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+- fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+- identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\nexec\n/bin/bash -lc \"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport importlib.util\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport numpy as np\n\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n\nsamples = 100\nlegacy_diff = 0\npartial_totals = []\nmax_depths = []\nframe_totals = []\nrepeat_excesses = []\nfor _ in range(samples):\n    inp = mod.generate_input()\n    squares = mod._detect_squares(inp)\n    azure = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s['c0']))\n    red = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.RED), key=lambda s: -int(s['c0']))\n    profile = mod._behavior_profile(azure, red, int(mod.get_bg(inp)))\n    if not np.array_equal(profile['fused_output'], profile['legacy_output']):\n        legacy_diff += 1\n    partial_totals.append(int(profile['azure_partial_pairs']) + int(profile['red_partial_pairs']))\n    max_depths.append(max(int(profile['azure_max_depth']), int(profile['red_max_depth'])))\n    frame_totals.append(len(squares))\n    counts = Counter([int(s['r0']) for s in azure] + [int(s['r0']) for s in red])\n    repeat_excesses.append(sum(v - 1 for v in counts.values() if v > 1))\n\nprint('samples', samples)\nprint('legacy_diff_rate', legacy_diff, '/', samples)\nprint('frame_total_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(frame_totals).items())))\nprint('max_depth_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(max_depths).items())))\nprint('partial_pair_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(partial_totals).items())))\nprint('repeat_excess_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(repeat_excesses).items())))\nprint('avg_partial_pairs', round(sum(partial_totals)/samples, 2))\nPY\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cecae3f8687e74c9fc61259fa8649238da2120f2\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,368 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+- fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+- identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\n succeeded in 2086ms:\nsamples 100\nlegacy_diff_rate 100 / 100\nframe_total_dist {10: 30, 11: 46, 12: 24}\nmax_depth_dist {3: 53, 4: 47}\npartial_pair_dist {6: 9, 7: 26, 8: 33, 9: 15, 10: 8, 11: 5, 12: 2, 14: 1, 16: 1}\nrepeat_excess_dist {4: 30, 5: 46, 6: 24}\navg_partial_pairs 8.24\n\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cecae3f8687e74c9fc61259fa8649238da2120f2\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,368 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+- fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+- identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\ncodex\n我发现一个可以顺手提高稳健性的点：把输入列间距从 5 改成 6，可以保留 1 格空隙，降低 5x5 滑窗误检风险，同时不影响层叠规则。我会补这个小修正，再复跑测试。\napply patch\npatch: completed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+  fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+  identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\n+                return None\n+\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+  fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+  identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\n+                return None\n+\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\nexec\n/bin/bash -lc '/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python -m py_compile /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py' in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\n succeeded in 0ms:\nexec\n/bin/bash -lc \"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python -c \\\"import numpy as np, importlib.util, time, sys\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\nok, fail = 0, 0\nt0 = time.time()\nfor i in range(50):\n    try:\n        inp = mod.generate_input()\n        out = mod.main(inp)\n        ok += 1\n    except Exception as e:\n        fail += 1\n        if fail <= 3: print(f'Trial {i}: {e}')\nelapsed = time.time() - t0\nprint(f'Success: {ok}/50, Time: {elapsed:.1f}s ({elapsed/50*1000:.0f}ms/call)')\nif ok < 40: sys.exit(1)\n\\\"\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+  fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+  identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\n+                return None\n+\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\n succeeded in 1437ms:\nSuccess: 50/50, Time: 1.2s (23ms/call)\n\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+  fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+  identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\n+                return None\n+\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\nexec\n/bin/bash -lc \"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport importlib.util\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport numpy as np\n\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n\nsamples = 100\nlegacy_diff = 0\npartial_totals = []\nmax_depths = []\nframe_totals = []\nrepeat_excesses = []\nfor _ in range(samples):\n    inp = mod.generate_input()\n    squares = mod._detect_squares(inp)\n    azure = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s['c0']))\n    red = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.RED), key=lambda s: -int(s['c0']))\n    profile = mod._behavior_profile(azure, red, int(mod.get_bg(inp)))\n    if not np.array_equal(profile['fused_output'], profile['legacy_output']):\n        legacy_diff += 1\n    partial_totals.append(int(profile['azure_partial_pairs']) + int(profile['red_partial_pairs']))\n    max_depths.append(max(int(profile['azure_max_depth']), int(profile['red_max_depth'])))\n    frame_totals.append(len(squares))\n    counts = Counter([int(s['r0']) for s in azure] + [int(s['r0']) for s in red])\n    repeat_excesses.append(sum(v - 1 for v in counts.values() if v > 1))\n\nprint('samples', samples)\nprint('legacy_diff_rate', legacy_diff, '/', samples)\nprint('frame_total_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(frame_totals).items())))\nprint('max_depth_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(max_depths).items())))\nprint('partial_pair_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(partial_totals).items())))\nprint('repeat_excess_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(repeat_excesses).items())))\nprint('avg_partial_pairs', round(sum(partial_totals)/samples, 2))\nPY\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\n+\n+e) Visual observability\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\n+\n+f) Visual cleanliness\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\n+  intended \"messier than base task\" look without turning the whole output into an\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\n+\n+g) Change inferability\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\n+  match a generalized legacy \"any overlap stacks inward\" output, so the D11 change is\n+  always active rather than accidental.\n+\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\n+  fused output against an \"any overlap\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\n+  identical.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+SQ = 5\n+AZURE = 8\n+RED = 2\n+GRID_H = 30\n+GRID_W = 42\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\n+\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\n+\n+\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\n+\n+\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\n+\n+\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\n+    return patch\n+\n+\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\n+\n+\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\n+                continue\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\n+                continue\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\n+                continue\n+            squares.append({\"frame\": frame_col, \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"patch\": patch})\n+    return squares\n+\n+\n+def _stack_side(\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\n+    width: int,\n+    edge_left: bool,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> list[dict]:\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\n+        r0 = int(square[\"r0\"])\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\n+        layer = None\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\n+                continue\n+            layer = int(idx)\n+            break\n+        if layer is None:\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \"stack depth exceeds canvas width\"\n+        placements.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(square[\"frame\"]),\n+                \"r0\": r0,\n+                \"c0\": int(square[\"c0\"]),\n+                \"dest_c\": dest_c,\n+                \"layer\": layer,\n+                \"patch\": square[\"patch\"],\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return placements\n+\n+\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\n+    bg: int,\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\n+    *,\n+    conflict_fn,\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+\n+    metrics = {\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": max((int(item[\"layer\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\n+        \"azure_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in azure_placed],\n+        \"red_layers\": [int(item[\"layer\"]) for item in red_placed],\n+    }\n+    return out, metrics\n+\n+\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\n+    rows = [int(square[\"r0\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\n+    count = 0\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\n+                count += 1\n+    return count\n+\n+\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\n+    return {\n+        \"fused_output\": fused_out,\n+        \"legacy_output\": legacy_out,\n+        \"azure_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\n+        \"red_partial_pairs\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\n+        \"azure_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"red_max_depth\": int(fused_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_azure_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"azure_max_depth\"]),\n+        \"legacy_red_max_depth\": int(legacy_metrics[\"red_max_depth\"]),\n+    }\n+\n+\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\n+    if top_band:\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\n+    else:\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\n+    patterns = {\n+        5: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\n+        ],\n+        6: [\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\n+        ],\n+    }\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\n+    return rows\n+\n+\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\n+        ordered.append(\n+            {\n+                \"frame\": int(frame_color),\n+                \"r0\": int(row),\n+                \"c0\": int(col),\n+                \"patch\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\n+            }\n+        )\n+    return ordered\n+\n+\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\n+\n+    Input structure:\n+    - Background is the most common color.\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\n+\n+    Rule:\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\n+\n+    Key insight:\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \"full row containment\" means matching the same row span, so exact\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n+    h, w = g.shape\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n+\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\"frame\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\n+        paste(out, item[\"patch\"], int(item[\"r0\"]), int(item[\"dest_c\"]), transparent=bg)\n+    return out\n+\n+\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\n+\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\n+\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\n+        return None\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\n+\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\n+\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\n+    if profile[\"azure_partial_pairs\"] + profile[\"red_partial_pairs\"] < 2:\n+        return None\n+    if max(int(profile[\"azure_max_depth\"]), int(profile[\"red_max_depth\"])) < 3:\n+        return None\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\"fused_output\"], profile[\"legacy_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\n+        paste(inp, square[\"patch\"], int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"]))\n+\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\"r0\"]), int(square[\"c0\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\n+        return None\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\n+                return None\n+\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\n+        return None\n+\n+    try:\n+        out = main(inp)\n+    except Exception:\n+        return None\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\"fused_output\"]):\n+        return None\n+    return inp\n+\n+\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\n+        if result is not None:\n+            return result\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\n succeeded in 2631ms:\nsamples 100\nlegacy_diff_rate 100 / 100\nframe_total_dist {10: 26, 11: 54, 12: 20}\nmax_depth_dist {3: 50, 4: 50}\npartial_pair_dist {6: 5, 7: 29, 8: 32, 9: 18, 10: 5, 11: 1, 12: 1, 13: 2, 14: 5, 15: 2}\nrepeat_excess_dist {4: 26, 5: 54, 6: 20}\navg_partial_pairs 8.5\n\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import random\n+import sys\n+from collections import Counter\n+from pathlib import Path\n+\n+import numpy as np\n+\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \"solvers\"\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\n+\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\n+\n+\n+\"\"\"\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\n+\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \"full containment\" between row spans\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\n+  Under the old rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\n+  Under the fused rule:\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\n+\n+b) Complexity calibration\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\n+  causes shifting.\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\n+\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\n+  symmetric \"fully nested\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\n+  equality.\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\n+\n+d) Placement legality\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by